finding: Medium — an accepted schema alias can become stale if its writable component object changes in place. `Component` is a mutable `Record<string, unknown>` and is not frozen; after `state.of(alias)` passes and stores it in `existing.schemas`, adding `alias.y = sparse(f32)` leaves `schemas.has(alias)` true, so the next `state.of(alias)` returns the old storage without `y` instead of rejecting/rebuilding. Repro on a6: it accepted the mutated alias and returned storage without the new field; the same repro on a2 rejected with `state.of: component schema changed`. The alias is otherwise scoped safely: each WeakSet is inside one State's `_storage`, a fresh State gets its own set, and `dispose()` clears the map. `WeakSet.has` itself does not allocate; the accessor test observes zero sorts for original/reloaded/rebuilt objects. The earlier six regressions and reorder fix pass. `check-imports` has 13 reds, identical to main. Packed AVBD and Agentic checks were rerun on a6 and pass; consumers are restored to locked `0.10.0-next.1` with manifests/locks unchanged.
